    "summary": "Explosive 'meat' sequence and soaring The Other One captured on crisp SBD",
    "blurb": "The Amsterdam show delivers a high‑energy second set highlighted by the classic meat sequence (Truckin’ → The Other One → Me And Bobby McGee → Wharf Rat). Jerry’s lead work shines on El Paso and The Other One, while Pigpen adds vocal heft on He's Gone. A superb soundboard makes this one of ’72’s most compelling recordings.",
    "review": "May 10, 1972 at the Concertgebouw finds the Dead in top form, weaving tight arrangements with adventurous improvisation. The first set rolls through a solid mix of classics and deep cuts—Bertha, Loser, and an emphatic El Paso—while the second set erupts into the legendary meat sequence that still feels fresh: Truckin’ launches into an explosive, jazzy Other One, segues into a soulful Me And Bobby McGee, then returns to The Other One before resolving on Wharf Rat. Jerry’s tone is both emotive and exploratory, Phil lays down powerful, jazz‑inflected bass lines, and Keith’s keyboards sit perfectly in the mix despite a slightly subdued presence. Pigpen’s vocals and organ rise above the rest on He's Gone and Next Time You See Me, and Bill Kreutzmann provides a tight rhythmic launchpad throughout the jams. The Kaplan soundboard capture is crisp and lively, with excellent instrument separation that lets every nuance of this high‑octane performance shine.",
